		{"id":"15bce32df090eb87","x":-400,"y":120,"width":800,"height":200,"type":"file","file":"🧠 What are the features of intelligence?/Overview of intelligence.md"},
		{"id":"b15fb411d952e5d4","x":-140,"y":20,"width":280,"height":50,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"How do we define intelligence?"},
		{"id":"7d6d450f7274ed04","x":493,"y":160,"width":347,"height":123,"type":"text","text":"> “Every aspect of learning or any other feature of intelligence can in principle be so precisely described that a machine can be made to simulate it.”\n\n  \n— John McCarthy et al., \"A Proposal for the Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence,\" 1955"},
		{"id":"7c89e4022dc01c20","x":529,"y":40,"width":276,"height":60,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"Why do we define intelligence?"},
		{"id":"47287678b3effba0","x":940,"y":155,"width":365,"height":134,"type":"file","file":"Further Reading/@mccarthyWHATARTIFICIALINTELLIGENCE2007.md"},
		{"id":"76acdc4ceb351ea3","x":529,"y":320,"width":272,"height":87,"type":"text","text":"We define intelligence so we may create systems that solve problems intelligently."}
	],
